1108 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The Stability of Passionate Metaphors\056 Abstract metaphors and figurative expressions appear more frequently than actual somatic descriptions\056 Across almost all periods\054 metaphors tend to lean toward high\055arousal\054 predominantly positive states\054 such as romantic love and creative excitement\056\nDramatic Events Reshaping the Language of Heat\056 During \050the wartime period \0501939\0231947\051\054 metaphorical anger emerges as \050a prominent new category\056 This suggests that during dramatic historical events\054 heat metaphors can be repurposed to express political and social rage\056 Furthermore\054 the political context surges to become the dominant semantic framework of this era\056\nSomatic Heat as a Stress Response\056 In times of relative stability\054 physical feverishness is often split between describing love and anxiety\056 However\054 during the turbulent first half of the 20th century\054 \034somatic romance\035 becomes less visible\056 Instead\054 physical heat appears to function as a language of discomfort\056)
     /K [ 8 ]
     /P 1109 0 R
     /Pg 1092 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

